From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: theodotdot <59372255+theodotdot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:55:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: chipone-icna35xx: keep the panel usable after a
 failed display off

A command-mode panel that loses a frame leaves the DSI controller with a
command DMA that never completes. Until something resets the controller, every
DCS write this driver makes times out, so ->disable() returns the accumulated
-ETIMEDOUT.

drm_panel_disable() keeps the panel marked enabled when ->disable() fails, and
drm_panel_enable() then skips the next enable, backlight included. The panel
only comes back on the second switch, once a disable has succeeded on a
controller msm_dsi_host_disable() has since reset.

Nothing in that failed disable is needed: ->unprepare() drives the reset line
and cuts the rails, and ->prepare() re-sends the whole init sequence, so the
panel comes up from scratch on the next enable whatever this call managed to
write.

Seen on the Ayn Thor (qcs8550-ayn-thor), niri driving the 1080x1920 command-mode
panel at 120Hz: one lost pingpong done on the DPU encoder left the panel showing
static, and the first switch to another mode and back logged

  dsi_cmds2buf_tx: cmd dma tx failed, type=0x39, data0=0x51, len=8, ret=-110
  panel-chipone-icna35xx ae96000.dsi.0: [drm:drm_panel_disable] failed to disable backlight: -110
  dsi_cmds2buf_tx: cmd dma tx failed, type=0x5, data0=0x28, len=4, ret=-110
  panel-chipone-icna35xx ae96000.dsi.0: sending DCS SET_DISPLAY_OFF failed: -110
  panel-chipone-icna35xx ae96000.dsi.0: Skipping enable of already enabled panel

Report the error and return 0, so one mode switch is enough and the next enable
runs its enable path.
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-chipone-icna35xx.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-chipone-icna35xx.c
@@ -346,7 +346,16 @@
 	mipi_dsi_dcs_enter_sleep_mode_multi(&dsi_ctx);
 	mipi_dsi_msleep(&dsi_ctx, 120);
 
-	return dsi_ctx.accum_err;
+	/*
+	 * Never fail the panel state machine: drm_panel_disable() keeps a panel
+	 * marked enabled after an error, so every later enable is skipped, the
+	 * backlight included.
+	 */
+	if (dsi_ctx.accum_err)
+		dev_warn(panel->dev, "display off failed: %d\n",
+			 dsi_ctx.accum_err);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int icna35xx_unprepare(struct drm_panel *panel)
